<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Shared MIME info for AsciiDoc and Markdown source documents.

  Teaches the system (via shared-mime-info / xdg-mime) that .adoc/.asciidoc
  files are "text/x-asciidoc" and .md/.markdown files are "text/markdown",
  rather than the generic "text/plain". Without this, our .desktop file's
  MimeType associations would silently never fire on systems whose
  shared-mime-info database doesn't yet include either type — file managers
  wouldn't surface "Open with Skrivro" in right-click menus.

  text/x-asciidoc is the de-facto MIME type for AsciiDoc source. The x-
  prefix marks it as unregistered with IANA (there's no official
  "text/asciidoc" yet — the IETF draft has been in limbo for years). Upstream
  shared-mime-info added an entry for this type in release 2.1 (2021), so
  on recent distros the AsciiDoc half is redundant. On older systems (or
  minimal installs without shared-mime-info 2.1+) it's the mechanism that
  makes "Open with Skrivro" show up in file manager right-click menus.

  text/markdown is IANA-registered (RFC 7763, 2016) and shipped in upstream
  shared-mime-info from release 1.6 (2015) onward. In theory most modern
  systems already know it. In practice some distros / minimal installs
  still report .md as text/plain, so we register it here for the same
  reason as AsciiDoc — the .desktop association needs the type to exist
  in the system's MIME database before file managers will use it.

  The sub-class-of text/plain declaration means each format is a specialized
  form of plain text. Tools that handle plain text still see .adoc / .md
  files as openable, but tools that specifically handle text/x-asciidoc or
  text/markdown (Skrivro) get priority. Clean polymorphism — we claim the
  specific type without blocking the generic handlers.

  Glob patterns deliberately match exactly the extensions Skrivro's
  detection logic recognizes (see resolveFormatForPath in tauri/src/io.ts):
  AsciiDoc gets .adoc and .asciidoc only; Markdown gets .md and .markdown
  only. Other variants (.asc for AsciiDoc; .mdown / .mkd / .mkdn for
  Markdown) are sometimes seen in the wild but not classified by Skrivro,
  so registering them here would mismatch — file manager would suggest
  "Open with Skrivro" for a file that Skrivro then classifies as text.
  Keep the two sources in sync if either ever changes.

  No magic block for Markdown because the obvious markers (a leading
  `# ` for an h1, or three dashes opening a YAML frontmatter block) are
  too weak as positive signals — both appear in plenty of plain text and
  YAML files. Extension-based detection is the standard for Markdown and
  what shared-mime-info upstream uses too. AsciiDoc's magic stays because
  :author: / :doctype: are AsciiDoc-specific enough to be safe.

  Install instructions (user-level, for the current user):

    mkdir -p ~/.local/share/mime/packages/
    cp tauri/com.skrivro.editor.xml ~/.local/share/mime/packages/
    update-mime-database ~/.local/share/mime/

  System-wide install (requires root):

    sudo cp tauri/com.skrivro.editor.xml /usr/share/mime/packages/
    sudo update-mime-database /usr/share/mime/

  After either, `xdg-mime query filetype somefile.adoc` should report
  text/x-asciidoc, and `xdg-mime query filetype somefile.md` should report
  text/markdown. Log out and back in (or restart the file manager) for
  the change to appear in "Open with..." menus.
-->
<mime-info xmlns="http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/shared-mime-info">
  <mime-type type="text/x-asciidoc">
    <comment>AsciiDoc source document</comment>
    <comment xml:lang="en">AsciiDoc source document</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="text/plain"/>
    <glob pattern="*.adoc"/>
    <glob pattern="*.asciidoc"/>
    <magic priority="50">
      <match type="string" offset="0" value="= "/>
      <match type="string" offset="0" value=":author:"/>
      <match type="string" offset="0" value=":doctype:"/>
    </magic>
  </mime-type>
  <mime-type type="text/markdown">
    <comment>Markdown source document</comment>
    <comment xml:lang="en">Markdown source document</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="text/plain"/>
    <glob pattern="*.md"/>
    <glob pattern="*.markdown"/>
  </mime-type>
</mime-info>
